// The enemy-type table. One row per type, and the only place a type is named.
//
// Not a header: it has no include guard and is meant to be included more than
// once, each time with ENEMY_TYPE defined to mean something different. The enum
// and the name table in EnemyTypes.h are both generated from these rows, and
// tools/enemy_types.py parses them for the Python side -- so the three lists
// that LevelLoader.cpp and export_level.py used to warn about keeping in step
// are now one list that cannot drift.
//
// One column, not two. A separate identifier and JSON string would let the two
// be renamed independently, which is the drift being removed; #Name gives the
// string for free.
//
// Adding a type is one row here. EnemyFactory's switch then warns about the
// missing case at the point you build, which is the intended and only
// remaining cost.
ENEMY_TYPE(Swordman)
